Day 9 — Step 7: Test automatic detection → database

Normally your CPU rule is:

if cpu > 90:
    return "HIGH"

Your computer may never reach 90% CPU during our test. So we will temporarily lower the threshold only for testing.

⚠️ Important

We will change it temporarily and then put it back to 90. This is only a controlled test.

Step 1 — Open threat_detector.py

In VS Code:

ACTDRS
└── detection
    └── threat_detector.py

Find:

if cpu > 90:

Temporarily change it to:

if cpu > 0:

So your CPU function becomes:

def detect_cpu(cpu):

    if cpu > 0:
        return "HIGH"

    return "NORMAL"

Do not change RAM or disk.

Save with:

Ctrl + S

Step 2 — Run ACTDRS

Open Ubuntu terminal.

Make sure you're here:

/home/priya/ACTDRS

Run:

python3 -m monitoring.system_monitor

Because your CPU will almost certainly be greater than 0%, you should see something similar to:

CPU Usage : 20.5%
CPU Status : HIGH

RAM Usage : 65.2%
RAM Status : NORMAL

Disk Usage : 34.9%
Disk Status : NORMAL

AI DECISION : CHECK_CPU

Most importantly, you should see the security event being saved if your system_monitor.py integration is correct.

Let it run for one or two scans only.

Then press:

Ctrl + C

Step 3 — Put the CPU threshold back

This is very important.

Go back to:

detection/threat_detector.py

